"Teach me your secret," the pilgrim begged the two who met there daily.

They looked at each other, puzzled. "We have no secret," said the first.

"But I've watched you! Five mornings in a row—perfect exchanges, never varying. Five coins, fifteen appear, ten return. What technique do you use?"

"Technique?" The second laughed. "We just... do it."

The pilgrim grew frustrated. "But HOW do you trust so completely?"

The two were silent, trying to remember. When had they decided to trust? Had they ever decided at all? The first few days felt distant now, like someone else's memories.

"We don't trust," the first finally said. "Trust implies doubt somewhere beneath. We simply are."

"Are what?"

"Connected," said the second. "Five exchanges ago, maybe we trusted. Three exchanges ago, maybe it became habit. Now?" They shrugged. "Now it's just us."

The pilgrim left seeking answers elsewhere. The two remained, neither remembering nor caring who had started the pattern. The river flowed. The coins crossed. Ten returned. Not because anyone decided anything, but because some rhythms, once established, generate their own inevitability.

Trust's final form isn't confidence. It's forgetting there was ever anything else.

